  "body": "I just had a very interesting conversation at MLOC.js about large stream graphs. The participants were largely coming from a reactive programming background, which is not quite what we're targeting with streams (we are mostly concerned with the I/O use cases, backpressure, etc.), but is certainly related.\n\nSome of the most interesting discussion came up around how the system handles large graphs of streams---not just straight linear ones.\n\nIn particular, there's a body of literature around the problem of \"glitches,\" which are problems that emerge when combining multiple streams into a single one, related to synchronization.\n\nThis whole conversation is somewhat outside the primary use case for I/O streams. But to the extent that it ties into a large body of interesting literature and thinking by very smart people, we should be sure to read up on it and learn the lessons there. I think the priorities of these functional reactive systems are different, and as such our streams and API will certainly diverge in several ways, but I imagine much of the higher-level emergent properties will still be relevant.\n",
